      en: "Victoria sits on the northeastern coast of Mahé, the main island, and is home to nearly a third of the Seychelles' total population, with roughly twenty-five thousand inhabitants. The settlement was founded in 1778 by French colonists under the name Établissement, and was renamed in 1841 in honor of Queen Victoria after the islands came under British rule. The downtown skyline is anchored by a small replica of London's Big Ben, the Clock Tower erected in 1903 in memory of the late queen. Notable sights include the vibrant Sir Selwyn Selwyn-Clarke Market, the brightly painted Arul Mihu Navasakthi Vinayagar Hindu temple, the Anglican Cathedral of St. Paul, and the Natural History Museum. Economically, Victoria is the beating heart of the archipelago: it hosts a deep-water port that handles one of the world's largest tuna canning operations, the Central Bank, the stock exchange, and most government ministries. The capital is also the transport hub from which ferries depart for Praslin and La Digue."
